  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/db/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_51.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "[RECAP: The whirling menace of the Human Top has proven himself much faster than Giant-Man, forcing the hero back into his downtown NYC lab in an effort to increase his speed.]\n\nHank, still training against a top-shaped robot, despairs of being fast enough to catch the real crook, while Dave Cannon hurls the pack of dynamite we saw him with at the end of last issue at an old, half-sunken tugboat in the East River, all apparently to help him \"commit the crime of the century\" by causing the authorities to rush to the scene and thereby distract them from where the crime will <i>really </i>occur.\n\nNews of the explosion in the East River soon reaches Hank and Jan (via television), but Hank correctly deduces that a \"worthless old tugboat\" can't have been the <i>true</i> target and suspects that it's just a distraction for something else.\n\nBut even as they prepare themselves to search \"the <i>west</i> side of town,\" they are interrupted by a mob of students who are part of the Giant-Man Fan Club.&nbsp; Yet the guys that storm the lab, claiming they're looking for a story for their school magazine, are all taken in by the Wasp's beauty and focus their cameras on her, much to Giant-Man's annoyance as they block his goal.\n\nHowever, it's easy enough for Giant-Man to bowl over the students, grab the Wasp, and head out to try and find the Human Top, which they soon do near the \"Federal Building.\"&nbsp; Moments later, Giant-Man races forward, prepared for round two!\n\nBut though he tries, Giant-Man's encounter with the twirling villain goes no better than his first attempt, and once again, he learns that the best he can do is cause massive property damage -- and get his leg stuck in a manhole -- as he blunders forward in vain, all in an attempt to grab the Top, who is still far too swift to be captured.\n\nEven the Wasp's attempt to slow the Human Top down by spraying water from a hydrant onto him (with the help of local policemen) proves ineffective, and it isn't long before the villain races away in possession of whatever it is he took from the Federal Building.&nbsp; Only then does Giant-Man admit that trying to match his speed will ultimately prove futile.\n\nSafe once again in his midtown apartment, Dave Cannon unmasks and holds up the \"civil defense plans\" he stole, planning to sell them to the \"Commies,\" who he's sure will pay him a good price.\n\nBut elsewhere, Giant-Man and the Wasp sit down with local authorities, who inform the pair that the stolen civil defense plans are even now being rendered obsolete, so no harm actually done in the end, but as the Human Top cannot possibly know this, they come up with a plan to try and catch the villain at the point he contacts the \"top Red agent in this city,\" who the feds claim has been \"under surveillance for months!\"\n\nHours later, just outside the apartment of the \"Red Agent,\" the feds detect the time and place for the planned exchange, and Giant-Man immediately heads next door and rounds up the Commie agents, ensuring that they end up in a federal prison, then heads out to make the rendezvous with the Human Top, while wearing a trench coat to conceal his identity.\n\nWhen the contact with the Human Top is finally made, Giant-Man reveals himself to his nimble opponent, who scoffs at the idea that this time will be any different than their previous encounters.&nbsp; But as the Top tries to flee, he soon finds that the entire area has been cordoned off by the police.&nbsp; Fences have been erected all around the area, leaving him little room to escape the lumbering Giant-Man.\n\nGiant-Man's size makes it a simple matter to scale the nearby buildings and, from there, leap down onto the fenced-in Human Top, who begins to tire.&nbsp; Furthermore, with the help of a little glue applied to his gloves, Giant-Man eventually transfers enough of it to the Top every time he manages to touch him, which eventually causes him to slow down just enough for Giant-Man to finally get hold of him.\n\nCaptured at last, the now-helpless Human Top is led away by the police, as Wasp exclaims how she doesn't mind getting \"stuck\" to Giant-Man.",
  "grade_one_liner": "The lead story regresses into formula and filler, saved only by a charming backup.\nYou turn the final page thinking, 'Well... at least <i>one</i> of the stories landed.'",
  "subtitle": "Spinning Into a Tired Old Trope",
  "review_text": "The feeling of futility at failing to round up the Human Top carries over from last issue, which at least feels earned after last month's cliffhanger.\n[ASSET: center | ContinuedFromLastIssue | width=67%]\n(Note that the \"downtown New York\" lab location seems a practical spot for Hank to use in order to be near the city's action, even though [LINK:1963:TTA_49_giant0|TTA #49|2026-12-19] showed us that he <i>also</i> owns a home in \"the New Jersey Palisades\" across the Hudson River.)\n\nThe Human Top tried to lure the police away from his true goal of stealing the \"civil defense plans\" to sell them to ([SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=who else?]) the [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Commies].&nbsp; (And we at least learn what the pack of dynamite that we saw on the final panel of <i>last</i> month's issue was for.)\n\n[ASSET: left | Students | width=12%] Even though Hank correctly deduces that an old tugboat couldn't <i>possibly</i> be the Top's true target, his plan to head to the other end of town is interrupted by a sudden surge of students who seem to have finally tracked him down, giving artist Jack Kirby an excuse to devote [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=an entire page] to showing Giant-Man's annoyance -- a full page of wheel‑spinning before the plot resumes.\n\n[ASSET: right | SpinningTooFast | width=14%] When Giant-Man and the Wasp <i>finally</i> extricate themselves from their adoring \"fan club,\" their second encounter with the Human Top goes no better than the first one, as seen in the previous issue.\n[BR]\n[BR] The Top is simply too fast to handle, forcing Hank to conclude that trying to match his speed will ultimately prove futile.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Almost without notice, Jack has quietly managed to advance the story from page 3 to the bottom of 9.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=And in so doing, we find ourselves right back where we started -- <b>nothing</b> of consequence has happened!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nI guess the Human Top <i>did</i> manage to steal \"civil defense plans\" to sell to the [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Commies,] but thanks to the ever-watchful federal agents, who have the Commie \"Red Agent\" under surveillance [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(convenient)], Giant-Man and the Wasp are able to cook up a new plan to intercept the Human Top at the point where he meets up with the foreign spies.\n[ASSET: center | ContactingTheRedAgent | width=67%]\n\nI feel obliged to remind readers that last December's [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1asm|2026-05-01] featured the Chameleon stealing \"missile defense plans,\" which he <i>also</i> tried to sell to the Commies.&nbsp; But in his case, he had to head to the \"waterfront\" to meet up with a Red submarine.\n\nBefore that, [LINK:1962:ST_101_torch0|ST #101|2026-04-01] (from over a year ago) showed <i>another</i> Commie sub -- clearly a favorite Cold War shortcut -- in a story involving the villainous Destroyer.\n\nEven <b>last month's</b> Vanisher was after \"continental defense plans,\" holding them for ransom, hoping the US government would pay to prevent them from being sold to the Communists (as seen in [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17]).\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=What's <i>with</i> these people anyway?&nbsp; Does our nation's defense matter so <i>little</i>?]\n\nWe're left to assume that, in the meantime, Commie spies have switched their operation over to having a single \"Red Agent\" available as a contact for anyone willing to divulge sensitive United States defense secrets!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Or that writer Stan Lee was hoping we'd simply <i>forget</i> these past encounters... <b>fat chance!</b>)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=Once again, the trope of selling secret plans to Communist \"boogey-men\" becomes a substitute for actually <i>inventive</i> storytelling, and a crutch relied on entirely too often.] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Sigh...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | NoPlaceToRun | width=40%] [ASSET: right | GlueOnGloves | width=9%] With only four more pages to go, Stan and Jack had to quickly bring the story to completion, meaning that it was time to fence in the desperate Human Top.\n[BR]\n[BR] And then, almost out of nowhere, they reveal that Giant-Man has been using glue to slow him down. (With the Wasp very naturally stating that she doesn't mind Hank's sticky fingers at <i>all</i>!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis issue regresses the book back to its ordinary dull status, and it was disappointing to see all the goodwill earned by the previous month's nice [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=TO BE CONTINUED] ending was almost immediately <i>squandered</i> one month later.\n\nThe formulaic nature of this episode was something you could practically tick off on a checklist:\n[HANG] * Giant-Man fan club [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head...still baffling)]\n[HANG] * Stolen military secrets\n[HANG] * The initial encounter with the villain going his way before anyone comes up with a better idea\n[HANG] * Giant-Man finding helpful allies in the police force (or feds)\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=All the while, Stan and Jack churn out page after page of mostly fluff, only to realize that they're on page 10 and need to wrap things up.]\n\nI tell you:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=there was very little else going for this.] The intrigue of the last month's backstory involving the Top completely disappears one month later, and the novelty of a villain who actually seems to have a chance against the hero falls back to Earth with a thud -- all that promise evaporating in an instant.\n\nAnd a Giant-Man fan club?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Really?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Note that they fixated on <b>Wasp</b> rather than Giant-Man... who <i>wouldn't?</i>)]\n\nYou don't see <i>Spider-Man</i> having a fan club -- and there's a <i>reason</i> for that.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(His name is J. Jonah Jameson...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<b>But the issue wasn't a total flop:</b>\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=On the positive side], the back-up story \"No Place to Turn!\" made me laugh.&nbsp; It was very reminiscent of the fan-made <i>Star Trek Continues</i> episode \"What Ships Are For.\"&nbsp; If you are a fan of the Original Series and haven't ever taken the time to check out that episode, it's worth a watch (and even features John de Lancie as one of its guest stars).&nbsp; I won't spoil the ending though.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Funny thing, of course, is that <i>this</i> issue's tale predates that episode by many <b>decades</b> -- yet the thematic overlap is uncanny.]\n\nFor the record, this issue's final grade received a half‑point bump [SPAN: severity=success; text=thanks <i>entirely</i> to this first backup story.]&nbsp; The main Giant‑Man feature wouldn't have earned a fully \"readable\" score on its own.\n\nAs for the second backup tale featuring [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale], it was a straight-up, run-of-the-mill \"tale to astonish,\" but Stan and Larry Lieber tried to spice it up a bit by having the Wasp tell it to a group of veterans or to orphans.&nbsp; But it's nothing more than dressing around a tale that might have just been too short to print on its own.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=As a result, I have elected to completely ignore it for the purposes of grading.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn the end, I continued mainly because I was already invested from last month, but there was little in this issue to justify that interest, and I kept turning the pages almost solely out of momentum, wanting to see it come to a resolution.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=But it was largely a disappointing end.]\n\nFurthermore, the Wasp is barely spotlighted -- reduced mostly to reaction shots and fan‑club fodder -- [SPAN: severity=info; text=and flirting of course...]",
  "highlight": "Giant‑Man, the Wasp, the Human Top, and one very unlucky Red Agent collide in a Cold War whirl."
}